finish('polynomial-time-quantum-dihedral-hidden-subgroup',
 id='TCS-6521',title='A polynomial-time quantum algorithm for the dihedral hidden subgroup problem',area='Quantum computation',criterion='resources',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Does there exist one uniform bounded-error quantum algorithm and constants C,c>0 such that, for every N≥2 and every oracle f hiding a subgroup H of D_N, the algorithm outputs generators of H with probability at least 2/3 using at most C(⌈log₂N⌉+ℓ+2)ᶜ total operations, where ℓ is the bit length of an oracle value?',
 definitions='The dihedral group D_N has 2N elements represented as pairs (x,b) with x∈ℤ/Nℤ and b∈{0,1}, and multiplication (x,b)(y,d)=(x+(−1)ᵇy mod N,b+d mod 2). Its identity is (0,0). The classical input is N in binary and the positive integer ℓ specifying the output-register width. An arbitrary subgroup H≤D_N is hidden by f:D_N→{0,1}^ℓ when f(g)=f(g′) if and only if gH=g′H, equivalently g⁻¹g′∈H. Thus distinct left cosets have distinct labels, whose bit patterns carry no promised structure. Quantum access is the reversible XOR oracle U_f|g,z⟩=|g,z⊕f(g)⟩, usable on superpositions; it acts as the identity on invalid group encodings. Each call costs one oracle operation. Count all other elementary quantum gates, measurements and classical bit operations as well, using a fixed universal gate set such as Clifford+T. Uniformity requires an effective polynomial-time classical controller, with no advice depending on N, H or f. There is no input truth table for f and no additional subset-sum, state-preparation or memory-access oracle. The output is a finite list of encoded group elements whose generated subgroup equals H; the empty list is allowed for H={1}. The success probability is over the algorithm’s internal randomness and measurements, for every legal hiding function separately. The displayed bound is a worst-case bound on total work, not merely query count or parallel depth. When ℓ is polynomial in log N, as in the usual oracle model, the target is polynomial time in log N.',
 answer_criterion='Construct and prove correct a quantum algorithm with the stated uniform polynomial total-work guarantee, or prove that no such algorithm exists. A polynomial number of oracle queries with superpolynomial postprocessing, a speedup using uncounted parallel hardware, or an algorithm requiring an additional subset-sum oracle does not suffice. Failure of one family of measurements or sieves is not a negative solution for all quantum algorithms.',
 context=[
 paragraph('Hidden subgroup problems ask a quantum algorithm to recover symmetry from equality patterns in a function. In an abelian group, Fourier sampling can turn those patterns into usable constraints, providing the framework for familiar quantum period-finding algorithms. The dihedral group adds a reflection to a cyclic rotation group and is among the simplest noncommutative settings in which the general polynomial-time method remains unresolved.','survey'),
 paragraph('A representative hard case has H=H_s={(0,0),(s,1)}, with an unknown s∈ℤ/Nℤ. The hiding promise says f(x,0)=f(x+s,1), and each restriction to b=0 or b=1 is injective. Recovering the subgroup is therefore recovering a cyclic hidden shift. The unrestricted subgroup problem reduces to this reflection case after handling its rotational part; the inherited Kuperberg paper uses the hidden-shift formulation for its main algorithm.','primary'),
 paragraph('A coherent oracle query followed by discarding the function value yields a coset state. In the reflection case it has the form (|0,x⟩+|1,x+s⟩)/√2 for an unknown random x. Fourier transformation of the cyclic coordinate produces a known random label k and a qubit with relative phase exp(2πiks/N). The phase contains information about s, but measuring that qubit does not simply reveal the modular product ks as a classical number.','survey'),
 paragraph('This is already enough information in a statistical sense: polynomially many queries suffice if computationally expensive processing is allowed. The main obstacle is implementing an effective collective extraction of the hidden shift with polynomial total work. A bound on oracle calls therefore cannot be substituted for a bound on running time. The card deliberately counts both quantum gates and classical postprocessing.','survey'),
 paragraph('Kuperberg’s first sieve gives time and query complexity 2^{O(√log N)}. It repeatedly combines phase states so that their labels become more useful for recovering the shift. This is a substantial speedup over generic classical approaches, yet it is superpolynomial in the input length n≈log N: the target here is n^{O(1)}, not 2^{O(√n)}.','sieve'),
 paragraph('The resource picture improved further. Regev obtains subexponential time with polynomial space, at a larger exponent involving √(log N log log N). Kuperberg’s later collimation sieve restores exp(O(√log N)) time while using only O(log N) quantum space, although classical storage remains subexponential. Small quantum memory and polynomial overall running time are different achievements.','primary'),
 paragraph('The lattice connection is important but needs its access-model qualification. Regev reduces a unique-shortest-vector problem to solving dihedral coset samples. Thus a polynomial algorithm that works from those samples would have consequences for lattice algorithms. An arbitrary hiding-function oracle provides a different interface, so one should not silently claim that every conceivable oracle-HSP algorithm automatically implements the coset solver required by that reduction.','lattices'),
 paragraph('An older positive claim remains visible in search results. Moore and Young’s February 2022 preprint claimed polynomial time for dihedral groups of power-of-two size, but was withdrawn on February 23. The withdrawal states that Lemma 4.3 is wrong and that the main algorithm needs the states asserted by that lemma. It is retained here as a documented withdrawn attempt, not a known algorithm.','withdrawn'),
 paragraph('A much more recent claim requires a separate status entry. Daniel Simon’s August 2026 preprint claims a polynomial-time dihedral coset algorithm, replacing a subset-sum step with a different erasure procedure. Its latest listed revision is August 17 and acknowledges a correction to the analysis as well as a new critical response. A valid coset algorithm of the claimed generality would settle the reflection case, making this directly relevant to the present question.','claim'),
 paragraph('Gupte, Ragavan and Zhandry’s response, revised September 1, gives an information-theoretic obstruction to the proposed algorithm. They show that its use of only a truncated portion of the classical Fourier labels leaves negligible advantage in guessing the secret’s least-significant bit. Their theorem covers a broader template containing the proposed procedure; it does not exclude all algorithms for the dihedral problem. The authors also supply a Lean formalization. This catalogue review checked the primary statements and repository description, but did not independently rerun that formalization.','refutation'),
 paragraph('Guo and Yang independently analyze the supporting lemmas. Their August 17 paper proves corrected statements while identifying a remaining independence assumption that the proposed choice of a partition does not supply. They explicitly conclude that establishing those lemmas does not establish correctness of the algorithm. The card therefore flags the recent claim as disputed rather than presenting polynomial time as established.','analysis'),
 paragraph('Positive results elsewhere among nonabelian groups continue to refine the boundary. Morales’ August 2026 algorithm handles certain scalar-action semidirect products with bounded generator rank and exponent-to-prime ratio polylogarithmic in group size, and another family with modular subgroup structure. For the ordinary dihedral family, the relevant ratio would be N/2, which does not meet that condition as N grows. These structural advances leave the unrestricted dihedral target intact.','recent'),
 paragraph('A solution would identify a new way to convert nonabelian quantum information into efficiently recoverable classical structure. A negative result would be equally consequential because polynomial query complexity is already available: it would isolate a genuine computational obstruction in processing the information. The significance comes from that algorithmic boundary, alongside the precise reductions to coset and lattice problems.')],
 why='A central test for quantum algorithms beyond abelian Fourier sampling. The information can be acquired with few queries, but extracting the subgroup efficiently remains a fundamental computational challenge with lattice-related consequences under the appropriate reductions.',
 importance=dict(score=94,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A basic nonabelian hidden-structure problem with a long-standing gap between polynomial information requirements and subexponential known algorithms.'),
 progress=[step('2003–2005','Kuperberg gives the first 2^{O(√log N)} quantum sieve; the journal version appears in 2005.','sieve'),step('2004','Regev develops the lattice-to-coset connection and, in separate work, a polynomial-space subexponential algorithm.','lattices'),step('2011–2013','Kuperberg’s collimation sieve achieves exp(O(√log N)) time with logarithmic quantum space and subexponential classical space.','primary'),step('23 February 2022','Moore–Young withdraw their claimed polynomial-time solution because a necessary lemma is incorrect.','withdrawn'),step('3–17 August 2026','Simon posts and revises a claimed polynomial-time dihedral coset algorithm; the latest listed revision acknowledges the critical response.','claim'),step('5 August 2026','Morales obtains polynomial algorithms for additional restricted nonabelian families, whose hypotheses do not cover all D_N.','recent'),step('17 August 2026','Guo–Yang give corrected lemma statements but identify an independence condition not supplied by the proposed algorithm.','analysis'),step('1 September 2026','Gupte–Ragavan–Zhandry revise their impossibility result for Simon’s proposed algorithm and a broader restricted template.','refutation')],
 references=[ref('primary','Another subexponential-time quantum algorithm for the dihedral hidden subgroup problem','Greg Kuperberg',2013,'https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3333','Inherited preprint posted 14 December 2011; TQC 2013, LIPIcs 22, 20–34; §§2–4 distinguish total work, quantum memory and hidden-shift access'),ref('sieve','A Subexponential-Time Quantum Algorithm for the Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem','Greg Kuperberg',2005,'https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/S0097539703436345','SIAM Journal on Computing 35(1), 170–188; main time and query bounds'),ref('survey','The dihedral hidden subgroup problem','Imin Chen; David Sun',2024,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09907','Journal of Mathematical Cryptology 18(1), DOI 10.1515/jmc-2022-0029; preprint v2 October 2022, §§4–6'),ref('space','A Subexponential Time Algorithm for the Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem with Polynomial Space','Oded Regev',2004,'https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0406151','Polynomial-space variant; time exp(O(√(log N log log N)))'),ref('lattices','Quantum Computation and Lattice Problems','Oded Regev',2004,'https://cims.nyu.edu/~regev/papers/quantum_average.pdf','SIAM Journal on Computing 33(3), 738–760; lattice reduction requires an algorithm operating on coset samples'),ref('withdrawn','A Quantum Polynomial-Time Solution to The Dihedral Hidden Subgroup Problem','Matthew Moore; Grace Young',2022,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.09697','WITHDRAWN v2, 23 February 2022; author explanation concerning Lemma 4.3'),ref('claim','A Polynomial-Time Quantum Algorithm for the Dihedral Coset Problem','Daniel R. Simon',2026,'https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1591','Received 3 August; latest listed revision 17 August 2026; disputed claim, with acknowledgment of ePrint 2026/1693'),ref('refutation','The ePrint:2026/1591 Quantum Algorithm Does Not Solve DCP','Aparna Gupte; Seyoon Ragavan; Mark Zhandry',2026,'https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1693','Received 15 August, revised 1 September 2026; primary abstract and author-hosted statement; accompanying Lean code linked by authors, not independently rerun here'),ref('analysis',"Rigorous Statements and Proofs of the Lemmas in Simon's Algorithm for the Dihedral Coset Problem and Their Underlying Hypothesis",'Yuchen Guo; Shuo Yang',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16598','17 August 2026; corrected lemmas and the remaining independence hypothesis'),ref('recent','The Hidden Subgroup Problem in Semidirect Products and Quasi-Hamiltonian Groups','Mauro E. S. Morales',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05321','5 August 2026; introduction and stated restrictions on exponent, generator rank and structured input')],
 status='uncertain',status_note='Reviewed through 11 September 2026. No verified polynomial-time solution is recorded. Simon’s August 2026 coset-algorithm claim is disputed: the September 1 Gupte–Ragavan–Zhandry response proves failure of the proposed template, and Guo–Yang identify an unsupported independence hypothesis. The review checked these primary statements without independently rerunning the Lean development. The 2022 Moore–Young claim was explicitly withdrawn.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
